      "post_id": "91008",
      "author_handle": "greline",
      "date_as_displayed": "Posted 8 years 3 months ago",
      "body": "My friend had a miscarriage on the first month and she wasn't even aware that she was pregnant until she had a heavy bleeding. Anyone here experienced the same?",
      "permalink": "https://www.justparents.co.uk/forums/topic-10722/#91008",
      "raw_file": "_raw/forums-deep/justparents/responses/2a75a12ac59baaf6e043.html",
      "page_url": "https://www.justparents.co.uk/forums/topic-10722/",
      "role": "original_post",
      "original_post_id": "91008"
    },
    {
      "post_id": "91499",
      "author_handle": "Ayanaben",
      "date_as_displayed": "Posted 7 years 5 months ago",
      "body": "It's quite common. But not everybody recognises it as miscarriage. 1 month miscarriage usually doesn't have any negative implications on future pregnancies. Often even ultrasound technicians won't be able to confirm it was a miscarriage. The embryo doesn't usually have a heartbeat in the first 4 weeks after the last period. It might even be too early to show on a pregnancy stick. Usually these miscarriages happen because the embryo isn't viable. If it becomes a baby, the baby will have serious issues. So it is best that you miscarry. Nature's way. But there are rare cases when you do stuff that can cause a miscarriage in the first month. Like high impact sports.\nI am talking of healthy women, with no significant complications, of course. The case will be different for others.",
      "permalink": "https://www.justparents.co.uk/forums/topic-10722/#91499",
      "raw_file": "_raw/forums-deep/justparents/responses/2a75a12ac59baaf6e043.html",
      "page_url": "https://www.justparents.co.uk/forums/topic-10722/",
